      "math_academy_id": "e-14640",
      "problem": "Find a fraction that is equivalent to $\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}}$ that does not contain a radical in the denominator.",
      "worked_solution": "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{2}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule to simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
